BT /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 56 760 Td (Dosecheck Rural) Tj T* () Tj T* (Dosecheck Rural) Tj T* (Executive Summary) Tj T* () Tj T* (Problem) Tj T* () Tj T* (Rural volunteer ambulance services carry patients on long transports, often 30 to 70 minutes to) Tj T* (the nearest hospital, through areas where cellular coverage is unreliable. Their crews are) Tj T* (trained but frequently stretched: many services run with two or three volunteers per call, and) Tj T* (the person documenting is usually also the person treating. In interviews with 19 crew members) Tj T* (and 6 service directors across four rural counties, 17 of 19 told us they still write vitals) Tj T* (and medication doses on paper, gloves, tape or the back of a hand during a call, and) Tj T* (reconstruct a formal record afterward. Eleven of the 19 said they had, at least once, been) Tj T* (unsure at hand-off whether a repeat dose had been given and when.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The consequences are concrete. Receiving nurses get verbal reports that omit doses or times.) Tj T* (Crews are unsure when a second dose is due. Services lose grant eligibility and state reporting) Tj T* (compliance when records are incomplete. Existing electronic patient care records exist, but) Tj T* (they were designed for paid urban services with reliable connectivity, dedicated tablets and) Tj T* (billing departments. Volunteer agencies mostly cannot afford them or cannot use them in a dead) Tj T* (zone.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Solution) Tj T* () Tj T* (Dosecheck Rural is an Android application, running on low-cost rugged tablets or crews' own) Tj T* (phones, that works fully offline. It does three things.) Tj T* () Tj T* (First, it records vitals and medications with large, glove-friendly buttons. A crew member taps) Tj T* (a medication and dose, and the app stamps the time and starts a configurable repeat-dose timer.) Tj T* (Vitals entry is designed around a typical sequence of heart rate, blood pressure, oxygen) Tj T* (saturation and respiratory rate, with trend lines drawn automatically.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Second, it generates a one-page hand-off report at the hospital door: a vitals trend, a) Tj T* (medication log with times and amounts, the crew's notes and the transport timeline. It can be) Tj T* (shown on screen, printed on a small portable printer or sent by secure link once a signal) Tj T* (returns.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Third, it syncs to a simple agency dashboard when connectivity is available, giving directors) Tj T* (complete run records for state reporting and grant applications.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The design principle is that the app must never need a signal and never slow the crew down.) Tj T* (Median time to log a medication in our prototype is 6 seconds.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Market) Tj T* () Tj T* (The United States has roughly 15,000 to 20,000 emergency medical services agencies, and) Tj T* (volunteer or mostly volunteer agencies make up a large share of them, concentrated in rural) Tj T* (areas. For planning purposes we use a conservative figure of 4,000 rural volunteer agencies) Tj T* (with an average of 2.5 ambulances each, or about 10,000 ambulances. At our target price this is) Tj T* (a serviceable market of roughly 3.5 million dollars annually. This is modest, and we state it) Tj T* (plainly: Dosecheck Rural is an initial wedge, not the full opportunity. The same offline hand-) Tj T* (off capability applies to rural career services, critical access hospital transfers and, later,) Tj T* (other settings with poor connectivity. We have not yet validated demand in those adjacent) Tj T* (segments.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Business Model) Tj T* () Tj T* (We sell an annual subscription of 350 dollars per ambulance, with a flat 750 dollars per year) Tj T* (for agencies running three or more ambulances. Onboarding is included: we configure medication) Tj ET
endstream
